<description>
Paint the inner foreground color into every background pixel that touches the enclosing frame only diagonally.

Input structure:
- The grid has one dominant background color and exactly two foreground colors.
- One foreground color is an enclosing frame: its bbox strictly contains the bbox of the other, inner color.

Rule:
- Keep all existing pixels unchanged.
- For each background pixel, if it is diagonally adjacent to the frame color and has no orthogonal adjacency to the frame color, recolor it to the inner color.

Key insight: The inner color marks the color to use, while the frame's diagonal-only background neighbors define the cells to add.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "an enclosing foreground frame and the inner foreground color inside its bbox",
  "transform": "recolor each background pixel that is diagonally adjacent to the frame and not orthogonally adjacent to it to the inner color; keep existing pixels unchanged"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 16x16, background 7
  colors: exactly 2 foreground colors (any from 0-9 excluding 7)
  frame:
    role: enclosing color whose bbox strictly contains inner color's bbox
    pixels: 4-21, density within own bbox 0.25-0.50
    bbox: 4-7 rows x 4-7 cols
    structure: sparse/airy pattern with bg gaps between pixels; e.g. lattice, diamond ring, or isolated corners
    8-connected: 1-4 components
    symmetry: LR symmetric around pattern center [observed]
  inner:
    role: enclosed color; small cluster inside frame bbox
    pixels: 2-4, bbox 2x2 or 3x1
    gap to frame bbox edge: >=1 on each side (strict containment)
  output_rule: |
    Keep all input pixels. For each bg pixel diagonally adjacent to frame
    but NOT orthogonally adjacent to frame or inner, recolor it to inner color.
    (New pixels are diag-only to ALL original fg pixels.)
    Output fg bbox expands exactly 1 pixel in each direction vs input fg bbox.
  margins: input fg bbox must have >=2 rows/cols of bg padding to all grid edges (so output has >=1)
  new_pixels_added: 12-15

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick 2 distinct fg colors and bg=7 on a 16x16 grid.
    2. Choose a frame pattern type (lattice with 2xN tile, diamond ring, corner-only, or similar sparse symmetric shape) with bbox 4-7 rows x 4-7 cols.
    3. Place a small inner cluster (2-4 pixels) strictly inside the frame bbox.
    4. Build the output by applying the diagonal-only fill rule to get new inner-color pixels.
    5. Verify output fg has >=1 margin from all grid edges; if not, shift pattern inward.
    6. The input is just the grid before the fill rule was applied (frame + inner on bg).
```
